#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""scripts/cdo-speed: the CDO speed and memory gate (docs/engine-limits.md L14).

Usage:
  scripts/cdo-speed <cdo-workspace> [--base REV] [--head REV] [--rounds N]
                    [--build-only] [--no-build] [--out DIR]
  scripts/cdo-speed --warm REV    (only build REV's binary into the cache; TLS-18:
                                   `scripts/merge-and-push` runs it after a landing, so
                                   the next gate finds its base cached)

It compares two `alsem` binaries, both built with the `release-fast` profile, on
a fixed set of CDO calls:
  head: the working tree (built in place, like every other gate), or `--head REV`;
  base: `--base REV`, by default the merge base of HEAD and `master`. When that
    is HEAD itself and the tree has no edits (a run on master), the default is
    HEAD's first parent instead, and the log says so: timing a tree against
    itself checks nothing.
A base binary is built from `git archive REV` and cached by commit under
`${ALSEM_SPEED_CACHE:-~/.cache/al-sem/cdo-speed}/bin/<sha>/`, so each master
commit is built once per machine, whatever worktree asks for it.

What runs: one `alsem mcp` server per side (the resident path agents use), asked
the same calls in turn (a fresh pair per warm-up pass; the last pair also runs the
rounds):
  warm-up (round 0, two passes, each on a fresh server pair; TLS-65): the first ask
    builds the CDO model (`build`), timed for each side with the other side's
    server closed (TLS-79: the other side's resident model made the second build
    cost x1.06-x1.29), then the first side's server is started again untimed;
    then one call of each tool builds the per-revision artifacts and the test
    universe (`warm-tests`). The second pass
    flips every call's side order, so each side is timed once going second (the
    second of a pair ran slower: ENG-81, ENG-97). Calibrated (TLS-79, 8 passes): per-pass
    build ratios x0.862-x1.029, a two-pass reading about ±8 %. Before each pass the host's CPU
    pressure (`some avg60`) must fall to CALM_PRESSURE; after CALM_WAIT_S (600 s) the
    pass runs anyway. Each timed block's CPU stall share (this container's `some total`
    over the block) is measured, and a block above CALM_PRESSURE (or a pass admitted by
    the timeout) labels the verdict "UNDER LOAD" with the shares: the same limits judge
    it, nothing is skipped (TLS-76);
  rounds 1..N (default 3, at most 5, as many as ROUND_ROUTINES has; a larger --rounds is
    refused before anything starts): one CDO routine per round, a different one each round
    (a resident answer is kept, so asking the same routine again measures
    nothing), asked prove may-commit, explain, digest, query effects, tests and
    impact. Round 1 is RunReportID, the routine of the round-4 slowdown.
The side order flips every round, and in the warm-up every call, so neither side
always goes first.

What is judged, per call, head over base:
  rounds: CPU time (user + system of the server and its engine children, from
    /proc) and wall time; the median of the per-round ratios must be at most
    CPU_LIMIT and WALL_LIMIT. The round total (every call of a round summed) is
    judged the same way. These calls run on about one thread (CPU time is about
    their wall time), so their wall limit says little about lost parallelism;
  warm-up: one sample per pass, the ratio the geometric mean of the
    passes, only the calls of at least WARM_MIN_SECONDS (the model build and the
    test universe); the verdict line shows each pass's ratio and the load range.
    CPU time at most WARM_CPU_LIMIT, and
    wall time at most WARM_WALL_LIMIT: a wider limit, since a single sample's
    wall time swings on a loaded machine (up to x1.32 measured). These are the
    parallel parts (~160 s of CPU in ~36 s of wall for the model build, ~135 s
    in ~18 s for the test universe), so their wall time is where a change that
    loses parallelism (same CPU, more wall) shows;
  peak memory: the sum of the peak RSS (VmHWM) of every server process, each
    read after every call (an engine child that exits mid-run still counts if
    it was alive at any read; one that lives and dies inside a single call does
    not), at most MEM_LIMIT.
A call whose base CPU time is under MIN_SECONDS is judged in the round total only
(a ratio of two tiny times is mostly noise). The CHANGELOG's engine-limits wave 1,
L14 entry gives the measured noise behind each limit. When the rounds disagree
about a call (some ratios over its limit, some not), more rounds run, up to
--max-rounds; then the median decides.

Instructions (TLS-83). Each timed call also counts the user-mode instructions of
the server and its engine children (`perf stat` attached for the call; both core
types on a hybrid CPU, unscaled). They resolve far better than CPU seconds (the
model build: typically ±0.1 %, one 0.8 % jump in 12 builds, against ±8 % for a
two-pass CPU reading; measured n = 6), but they count work, not time: they do not see memory
stalls, cache misses or lost parallelism, so CPU and wall keep judging. Each
verdict line shows the call's instruction ratio, and the PASS/FAIL line the model
build's and the round total's. A ratio over INSTR_LIMIT (x1.02) is reported as an
ADVISORY and never fails the run: it becomes a failing limit only by an owner's
decision on data from real landings. Without perf (or CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0) the run
says "instructions: NOT counted" and judges CPU and wall alone.

Output equality (the answer's hash) is reported (a branch may change answers on
purpose), never judged here: the CDO goldens do that. A call that exits non-zero
or fails in the engine, on either side, fails the run at once, naming the call,
its exit code and the first lines of its stderr: every call exits 0 on CDO, and
a ratio of failed calls (a wrong workspace answers in 0.01 s) measures nothing
(TLS-10: a run on a wrong workspace once printed PASS).

Exit: 0 pass, 1 fail (slower or larger, or a call failed), 2 cannot run (usage,
build, an MCP request that failed or timed out). A run under load exits as any other,
labelled. Linux only (/proc).
"""
import argparse
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import select
import shutil
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import signal
import tempfile
import time

# The limits, head over base. See the module doc.
CPU_LIMIT = 1.20
WALL_LIMIT = 1.30
# The warm-up's calls have one sample per pass: wider limits. Its wall limit is
# above the worst single sample against the same binary (x1.32: the model build
# beside a 22-core build; x1.31 beside 22 busy loops) and far below what losing
# their parallelism costs (on one core, from their CPU over wall: the model
# build about x4.4, the test universe about x7.5).
WARM_CPU_LIMIT = 1.30
WARM_WALL_LIMIT = 1.60
MEM_LIMIT = 1.10
MIN_SECONDS = 2.0
# A warm-up call is judged when its base CPU time is at least this: the model
# build (~160 s CPU) and the test universe (~135 s), not the 1-5 s calls whose
# single sample swung up to x1.19 against the same binary under load.
# (CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS: a test hook, so a stub's short calls are judged.)
WARM_MIN_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS", "20.0"))
# ⟨TLS-65⟩ The warm-up is timed twice, on two fresh server pairs, the second
# with every call's side order flipped: a call's ratio is the geometric mean of
# the two (it cancels a factor k on one pass and 1/k on the other exactly).
# One sample in a fixed order always timed head's model build second; the side
# built second was measured once at x1.167 for a binary against itself at load
# ~9 (ENG-81; not reproduced in TLS-65's own loaded run, x1.02), and one quiet
# TLS-65 run read x1.096 with head second against x1.004 flipped.
WARM_SAMPLES = 2
# ⟨TLS-76⟩ CPU pressure, not the 1-min load average (TLS-65's signal), which
# counts other projects' runnable work on this shared host: it read 50-230 while
# the CPU was ~78 % idle and stopped every gate (owner q-20261115T181612-b2891501).
# Two uses (reviews r1, Fable and sol: a cgroup's pressure counts only its own
# tasks' stalls, so an idle container reads ~0 whatever the host does):
# - Admission: before each warm-up pass, the HOST's `some avg60`
#   (`/proc/pressure/cpu`; a minute, so one dip in bursty load does not pass) must
#   be at most CALM_PRESSURE; after CALM_WAIT_S the pass runs anyway. This only
#   lowers the odds of a loaded run.
# - The label: the share of each timed block (each warm-up pass, the rounds) in
#   which some task of THIS container waited for a CPU, from the cgroup's
#   `some total=` stall microseconds at its start and end (exact over the block,
#   no averaging window). A block above CALM_PRESSURE, or a pass admitted only by
#   the timeout, labels the verdict "UNDER LOAD" with the shares; the same limits
#   judge it (principle 6: label, never hide; 9: never weaken a gate).
# Calibration: see the CHANGELOG's TLS-76 entry.
CALM_PRESSURE = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_PRESSURE", "15"))
CALM_WAIT_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_WAIT_S", "600"))
CALM_POLL_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_POLL_S", "10"))
HOST_PRESSURE = "/proc/pressure/cpu"
OWN_PRESSURE = ("/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.pressure", "/proc/pressure/cpu")


TEST_HOOKS = ("CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS", "CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE", "CDO_SPEED_PRESSURE_FILE",
              "CDO_SPEED_HOST_PRESSURE_FILE", "CDO_SPEED_CALM_PRESSURE", "CDO_SPEED_CALM_WAIT_S",
              "CDO_SPEED_CALM_POLL_S", "CDO_SPEED_PERF")


def load_1min():
    """The 1-min load average, recorded only (CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE: a test hook read instead)."""
    path = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE")
    if path:
        with open(path) as f:
            return float(f.read().split()[0])
    return os.getloadavg()[0]


def psi_some(paths, key):
    """The `some` line's `key` (`avg10`, `avg60`, `total`) from the first readable
    PSI file of `paths`, as (value, path), or (None, None)."""
    for path in paths:
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                for line in f:
                    fields = line.split()
                    if fields and fields[0] == "some":
                        for kv in fields[1:]:
                            if kv.startswith(key + "="):
                                return float(kv[len(key) + 1:]), path
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    return None, None


def host_files():
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_HOST_PRESSURE_FILE")
    return [hook] if hook else [HOST_PRESSURE]


def own_files():
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_PRESSURE_FILE")
    return [hook] if hook else list(OWN_PRESSURE)


def cpu_pressure():
    """This container's `some avg10` (recorded per call), as (value, path)."""
    return psi_some(own_files(), "avg10")


class Stalls:
    """This container's CPU stall share over one timed block: the `some total=`
    microseconds at its start and end, over its wall time."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.total0, _ = psi_some(own_files(), "total")
        self.t0 = time.monotonic()

    def share(self):
        """The share in percent, or None when no total can be read."""
        total, _ = psi_some(own_files(), "total")
        wall = time.monotonic() - self.t0
        if total is None or self.total0 is None or wall <= 0:
            return None
        return 100.0 * (total - self.total0) / 1e6 / wall


def shares_text(blocks):
    """`warm-up 1 3.2 %, ...` (or "not readable" for a block with no total)."""
    return ", ".join(f"{name} {'not readable' if s is None else f'{s:.1f} %'}"
                     for name, s in blocks) or "none timed"


def under_load_label(calm, blocks):
    """The verdict's label: " UNDER LOAD: ..." when a timed block's stall share
    passed CALM_PRESSURE or a pass was admitted only by the timeout; a note when
    the pressure could not be read; else ""."""
    high = [name for name, s in blocks if s is not None and s > CALM_PRESSURE]
    timed_out = [i + 1 for i, (_, loaded) in enumerate(calm) if loaded]
    if high or timed_out:
        why = []
        if high:
            why.append(f"stall share above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % in {', '.join(high)}")
        if timed_out:
            why.append(f"host pressure above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % for {CALM_WAIT_S:.0f} s "
                       f"before warm-up pass(es) {', '.join(map(str, timed_out))}")
        return f" UNDER LOAD: {'; '.join(why)} (shares: {shares_text(blocks)})"
    if any(loaded is None for _, loaded in calm) or any(s is None for _, s in blocks):
        return f" (CPU pressure not fully readable: shares {shares_text(blocks)})"
    return ""


def wait_calm(what):
    """Wait until the host's `some avg60` is at most CALM_PRESSURE. Returns (the
    pressure then, True when the pass was admitted only by the CALM_WAIT_S timeout,
    None when no host pressure can be read)."""
    start = time.monotonic()
    said = False
    while True:
        p, source = psi_some(host_files(), "avg60")
        if p is None:
            log(f"{what}: no host CPU pressure readable ({', '.join(host_files())}): not checked")
            return None, None
        if p <= CALM_PRESSURE:
            if said:
                log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60, {source}), calm enough after "
                    f"{time.monotonic() - start:.0f} s")
            return p, False
        waited = time.monotonic() - start
        if waited >= CALM_WAIT_S:
            log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60) stayed above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % "
                f"for {waited:.0f} s: running anyway, UNDER LOAD")
            return p, True
        if not said:
            log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60, {source}) is above "
                f"{CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} %: waiting (up to {CALM_WAIT_S:.0f} s)")
            said = True
        time.sleep(CALM_POLL_S)

RRID = "CDO E-Mail Handler::RunReportID"
ISSUE = "CDO Issue Document::IssueReminder"
TELEMETRY = "CDO Telemetry::UpdateLastUpload"

# One routine per round (round 1 first); --max-rounds is at most this many.
# Each one's first prove costs 10-20 s on master (release-fast, 2026-11-09): a
# routine whose answer takes 1-2 s says little about a slowdown.
ROUND_ROUTINES = [
    RRID,
    ISSUE,
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::DocEMailSend",
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::DocSave",
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::RunReport",
]


def calls_for(routine):
    """(id, tool, arguments) for one routine. Prove first: explain reuses its search."""
    q = routine.replace("::", ".")
    return [
        ("prove", "alsem_prove",
         {"routine": routine, "question": "may-commit", "deterministic": True}),
        ("explain", "alsem_explain", {"routines": [routine]}),
        ("digest", "alsem_digest", {"routines": [routine], "deterministic": True}),
        ("query", "alsem_query_effects", {"routine": q, "deterministic": True}),
        ("tests", "alsem_tests", {"routines": [routine]}),
        ("impact", "alsem_impact", {"routines": [routine]}),
    ]


# Round 0: `build` is the first ask (the model); the rest build what each tool
# needs once per revision (the test universe for `tests`).
WARM_UP = [("build", "alsem_explain", {"routines": [TELEMETRY]})] + [
    (f"warm-{cid}", tool, args) for cid, tool, args in calls_for(TELEMETRY)]

CALL_TIMEOUT = 900
TICK = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK") if hasattr(os, "sysconf") else 100


def log(msg):
    print(f"cdo-speed: {msg}", flush=True)


def git(repo, *args):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", repo, *args], check=True, capture_output=True,
                          text=True).stdout.strip()


def timed_hold(repo):
    """⟨TLS-89⟩ Mark the CDO slot's hold timed for the rest of this run (after its
    builds, before the first timed call), so no build runs beside the timing: the
    guard refuses new ones, and cdo-slot waits for running ones and refuses (exit 6)
    when they do not end. Inside a hold (cdo-gate's or land's token, `cdo-slot run`,
    or this Claude session's hand hold) it shares it; outside any it takes the slot
    itself, waiting its turn (review r1, sol: a run with no token went unguarded).
    Bound to this process: the mark, and a hold it took, end with it."""
    rc = subprocess.run(["bash", os.path.join(repo, "scripts", "cdo-slot"), "acquire",
                         os.environ.get("CDO_SLOT_OWNER", "cdo-speed"), "cdo-speed timing",
                         "--wait", "--bind", str(os.getpid()), "--timed"]).returncode
    if rc == 6:
        log("builds still ran when the timing was to begin (scripts/cdo-slot --timed): "
            "timed nothing")
        sys.exit(2)
    if rc not in (0, 4):
        log(f"the timed CDO slot hold was not taken (cdo-slot exit {rc})")
        sys.exit(2)


def child_env():
    # Nothing that changes the engine's behaviour or adds tracing cost.
    return {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items()
            if not k.startswith("ALSEM_") and k != "C1_CONE_CENSUS"}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binaries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def target_dir(repo):
    return os.environ.get("CARGO_TARGET_DIR") or os.path.join(repo, "target")


def gate_release_dir(repo):
    """The folder `scripts/ci-steps`' optimized build (`build` / `perf-bounds`) uses:
    `<target>/gate-release`, spelled as ci-steps spells it (TLS-09)."""
    return os.path.join(target_dir(repo), "gate-release")


def build_working_tree(repo, logf):
    """Build the working tree's alsem (release-fast) in ci-steps' optimized build folder;
    return its path. ⟨TLS-33⟩ A landing gate runs `ci-steps all` first, which builds the
    same profile and features there, so cargo finds every artifact fresh and builds nothing
    (in the main target it rebuilt al-sem: 255 s on cdo-gate's critical path). An edit
    since is rebuilt by cargo's own freshness check; with no ci-steps build first (a
    scheduled run) it builds as before, in that folder. ci-steps' build and this one never
    run at once (`ci-steps all` ends before `cdo-gate` starts), and cargo's build-folder
    lock would make the second wait if they did."""
    log("building the working tree (release-fast, alsem)")
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"] = gate_release_dir(repo)
    rc = subprocess.run(["cargo", "build", "--profile", "release-fast", "--bin", "alsem"],
                        cwd=repo, env=env, stdout=logf, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT).returncode
    if rc != 0:
        raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: the working tree's build failed (log: {logf.name})")
    log(f"  built in {time.monotonic() - t0:.0f} s")
    return os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), "release-fast", "alsem")


def default_base(repo, head_rev):
    """The merge base of HEAD and master; on master itself with no edits (a
    scheduled run, or the post-merge check at the merge commit) that is the tree
    being timed, so HEAD's first parent instead."""
    base = git(repo, "merge-base", "HEAD", "master")
    if head_rev is not None or base != git(repo, "rev-parse", "HEAD"):
        return base
    if git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no"):
        log("the merge base is HEAD; timing the edits against HEAD")
        return base
    parent = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "HEAD^1")
    log("****************************************************************")
    log("**  HEAD is the merge base and the tree has no edits: timing    **")
    log(f"**  HEAD against its first parent {parent[:10]} instead.       **")
    log("****************************************************************")
    return parent


def cache_root():
    return os.environ.get("ALSEM_SPEED_CACHE") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cache", "al-sem", "cdo-speed")


TREE_ENTRY_DAYS = 7


def grammar_dir(repo, archive):
    """The grammar clone a build uses: `TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH` when set; else the
    working tree's own `tree-sitter-al` (al-syntax's build.rs fallback) or, for an
    archive build (`cached_binary`), the main checkout's."""
    if os.environ.get("TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"):
        return os.environ["TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"]
    if archive:
        return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(
            git(repo, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"),
            "..", "tree-sitter-al"))
    return os.path.join(repo, "tree-sitter-al")


# Environment that changes what a build compiles, beyond the sources and the
# grammar: any of these set means no key (review r1-r2, sol).
BUILD_ENV_EXACT = ("RUSTFLAGS", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS", "RUSTC", "RUSTC_WRAPPER",
                   "RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER", "CC", "CXX", "CFLAGS", "CXXFLAGS", "CPPFLAGS",
                   "LDFLAGS", "AR",
                   # cc-rs's host and target spellings (review r3, sol); by name, as a
                   # `HOST_` prefix would also catch `HOSTNAME`.
                   "HOST_CC", "HOST_CXX", "HOST_CFLAGS", "HOST_CXXFLAGS", "HOST_AR",
                   "TARGET_CC", "TARGET_CXX", "TARGET_CFLAGS", "TARGET_CXXFLAGS", "TARGET_AR",
                   "CRATE_CC_NO_DEFAULTS",
                   # Review r1, Opus: another compiler than the tree's pin, incremental
                   # code in release-fast, a value the root build.rs bakes into the binary.
                   "RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN", "CARGO_INCREMENTAL", "AL_CH_TELEMETRY_CONNECTION_STRING")
BUILD_ENV_PREFIXES = ("CARGO_PROFILE_", "CARGO_BUILD_", "CARGO_TARGET_", "CFLAGS_",
                      "CXXFLAGS_", "CC_", "CXX_", "AR_", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS")
# Where and how fast a build runs, not what it compiles.
BUILD_ENV_HARMLESS = ("CARGO_TARGET_DIR", "CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR", "CARGO_BUILD_JOBS")


def build_overrides(repo):
    """What, besides the sources and the grammar, could change a build here: build
    environment, or a Cargo config file in a folder above the repo (the repo's own
    `.cargo` is in its tree; the user's `$CARGO_HOME` applies to every build alike).
    Empty when there is none."""
    found = [k for k in os.environ if os.environ[k] and k not in BUILD_ENV_HARMLESS and (
        k in BUILD_ENV_EXACT or k.startswith(BUILD_ENV_PREFIXES))]
    cargo_home = os.path.realpath(os.environ.get("CARGO_HOME")
                                  or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cargo"))
    d = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(repo))
    while True:
        for name in ("config", "config.toml"):
            p = os.path.join(d, ".cargo", name)
            if os.path.exists(p) and os.path.realpath(os.path.dirname(p)) != cargo_home:
                found.append(p)
        parent = os.path.dirname(d)
        if parent == d:
            return found
        d = parent


def build_key(repo, tree, grammar):
    """⟨TLS-34⟩ What a kept binary is keyed by: the source tree and the grammar
    clone's commit (the grammar is an unpinned clone outside git, review r1, sol).
    `None` when the build's inputs are not those two alone: a grammar clone given
    by a relative path (git and the build would resolve it from different folders),
    with local changes or untracked files (an added `scanner.c` is compiled), or
    with no commit; or build overrides (`build_overrides`). No binary is kept or
    seeded then."""
    if not os.path.isabs(grammar) or build_overrides(repo):
        return None
    try:
        rev = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", grammar, "rev-parse", "HEAD"], capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
        dirty = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", grammar, "status", "--porcelain",
                                "--untracked-files=normal"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                               check=True).stdout.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):
        return None
    return None if dirty or not rev else f"{tree}-g{rev[:12]}"


def working_tree_key(repo):
    """The build key of the working tree when it is clean (no tracked change and
    no untracked file outside .gitignore, as ci-steps' gate-pass record asks);
    `None` otherwise."""
    if git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=normal"):
        return None
    return build_key(repo, git(repo, "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"),
                     grammar_dir(repo, archive=False))


def record_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before):
    """⟨TLS-34⟩ Keep the working tree's release-fast binary in the cache under its
    build key, so `cached_binary` can seed a commit with that tree (and grammar)
    instead of building it. Only when the key taken before the build is the key
    after it (the sources did not change around the build), and the binary is
    copied into the cache before waiting for the cache lock (a later build into
    the same folder cannot swap it; review r1, sol). It differs in bytes from an
    archive build (the build folder's path is embedded), not in what it runs.
    Entries older than TREE_ENTRY_DAYS go (a branch that never lands).

    The binary is snapshotted first, as soon as cargo returns, before any check, and
    the caller holds `.cdo-speed-build.lock` in that folder from the first key through
    this snapshot, so no other cdo-speed builds there in between (review r2-r3, sol).
    Limit: ci-steps' own build and perf-bounds also use `<target>/gate-release`
    without that lock; a landing runs `ci-steps all` to its end before `cdo-gate`
    starts, so they do not overlap there.

    Keeping is an optimization: with no key it does nothing, and a file error only
    logs. It never fails the speed check, whose exit 1 means "slower" (review r1,
    Opus)."""
    if key_before is None:
        log("the work tree has changes, a modified grammar or build overrides: its "
            "binary is not kept for a later base")
        return
    try:
        _keep_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before)
    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError) as e:
        log(f"the working tree's binary was not kept (it only saves a later build): {e}")


def _keep_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before):
    root = cache_root()
    trees = os.path.join(root, "trees")
    os.makedirs(trees, exist_ok=True)
    # A unique name (review r1, Opus); a leftover of a killed run is pruned below.
    fd, snapshot = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=trees, prefix=".incoming-")
    os.close(fd)
    shutil.copy2(built, snapshot)
    key = working_tree_key(repo)
    if key != key_before:
        os.remove(snapshot)
        log("the sources changed during the build: its binary is not kept for a later base")
        return
    dst = os.path.join(trees, key, "alsem")
    with open(os.path.join(root, "lock"), "w") as lock:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        cutoff = time.time() - TREE_ENTRY_DAYS * 86400
        for name in os.listdir(trees):
            path = os.path.join(trees, name)
            if name.startswith(".incoming-"):
                # Another run's snapshot in flight is minutes old at most.
                if path != snapshot and os.path.getmtime(path) < time.time() - 86400:
                    os.remove(path)
            elif os.path.getmtime(path) < cutoff:
                shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)
        if os.path.exists(dst):
            os.remove(snapshot)
        else:
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst), exist_ok=True)
            os.replace(snapshot, dst)
    log(f"kept the working tree's binary for {key[:10]}…{key[-13:]} (a later base of that "
        f"tree and grammar builds nothing)")


def cached_binary(repo, rev, logf, extra=()):
    """The release-fast alsem of commit `rev`, built once per commit and cached.
    ⟨TLS-61⟩ `extra` names more binaries of the same build (`aldump` for
    scripts/cdo-before-after), kept beside alsem in the same commit folder; only
    the missing ones are built."""
    sha = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{rev}^{{commit}}")
    root = cache_root()
    exe = os.path.join(root, "bin", sha, "alsem")
    os.makedirs(root, exist_ok=True)
    # One build at a time per cache (two gates may want the same commit).
    with open(os.path.join(root, "lock"), "w") as lock:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        missing = [b for b in ("alsem", *extra)
                   if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(exe), b))]
        if not missing:
            log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: cached binary {exe}"
                + (f" (and {', '.join(extra)} beside it)" if extra else ""))
            return exe, sha
        # ⟨TLS-34⟩ A gate that timed this commit's exact tree left its binary
        # (`record_tree_binary`): a landing's merge commit has the tree its gate
        # tested, so the warm step after a landing builds nothing.
        key = build_key(repo, git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{sha}^{{tree}}"),
                        grammar_dir(repo, archive=True))
        seeded = key and os.path.join(root, "trees", key, "alsem")
        if "alsem" in missing and seeded and os.path.exists(seeded):
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(exe), exist_ok=True)
            tmp = exe + ".tmp"
            shutil.copy2(seeded, tmp)
            os.replace(tmp, exe)
            log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: seeded from a gate's build of its tree ({key[:10]}…) "
                f"(no build) at {exe}")
            missing.remove("alsem")
            if not missing:
                return exe, sha
        src = os.path.join(root, "build", "src")
        log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: building {', '.join(missing)} (once per commit) in {src}")
        shutil.rmtree(src, ignore_errors=True)
        os.makedirs(src)
        # `tar -m`: every file gets the current time, so cargo rebuilds the
        # workspace crates. An archive keeps commit times, and an older commit's
        # files would look older than the last build's outputs (a stale binary).
        archive = subprocess.Popen(["git", "-C", repo, "archive", "--format=tar", sha],
                                   stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
        untar = subprocess.run(["tar", "-x", "-m", "-C", src], stdin=archive.stdout)
        archive.stdout.close()
        if archive.wait() != 0 or untar.returncode != 0:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: git archive {sha} failed")
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"] = os.path.join(root, "build", "target")
        if not env.get("TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"):
            # The archive has no grammar clone; the checkout's own is the one
            # this repository builds against.
            # The same clone `build_key` reads for the seeding key (review r1, Opus).
            env["TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"] = grammar_dir(repo, archive=True)
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        bins = [a for b in missing for a in ("--bin", b)]
        rc = subprocess.run(["cargo", "build", "--profile", "release-fast", *bins],
                            cwd=src, env=env, stdout=logf, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT).returncode
        if rc != 0:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"cdo-speed: building {rev} ({sha[:10]}) failed (log: {logf.name}). A grammar "
                "change since that commit stops its build (node-types.sha256); then pass a "
                "--base that builds against the current grammar, or skip the speed check.")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(exe), exist_ok=True)
        for b in missing:
            dst = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(exe), b)
            tmp = dst + ".tmp"
            shutil.copy2(os.path.join(env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"], "release-fast", b), tmp)
            os.replace(tmp, dst)
        log(f"  built in {time.monotonic() - t0:.0f} s")
        return exe, sha


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measuring
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def proc_status_kib(pid, key):
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/status") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith(key + ":"):
                    return int(line.split()[1])
    except OSError:
        pass
    return 0


def proc_cpu_seconds(pid):
    """User + system time of `pid` and of its children it has waited for."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            stat = f.read()
        fields = stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()
        # utime, stime, cutime, cstime (fields 14-17 of proc(5)).
        return sum(int(x) for x in fields[11:15]) / TICK
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0.0


def descendants(pid):
    """Every live descendant of `pid` (from /proc's parent links)."""
    parents = {}
    for d in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not d.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{d}/stat") as f:
                stat = f.read()
            parents.setdefault(int(stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()[1]), []).append(int(d))
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
    out, todo = [], [pid]
    while todo:
        kids = parents.get(todo.pop(), [])
        out.extend(kids)
        todo.extend(kids)
    return out


# ⟨TLS-83⟩ Instructions: user-mode instructions retired per call, next to its
# CPU seconds. On server1 (2026-11-18, one binary against itself, 3 runs) the
# model build's ratio held within about ±0.1 % (one 0.8 % jump in 12 builds;
# n = 6), against sd(log) 0.084 in CPU seconds: ~27x; judged calls ~40-400x.
# They do
# not see memory stalls, cache misses or lost parallelism, so CPU and wall keep
# judging; the instruction limit is ADVISORY (reported, never failing) until
# real landings show what it would catch (docs/superpowers/notes/
# 2026-11-18-tls83-speed-resolution.md). The events in use: both core types'
# counters on a hybrid CPU, else the plain one; empty when perf cannot count.
INSTR_EVENTS = ()
INSTR_WHY = ""
INSTR_LIMIT = 1.02
# How long a call waits for perf to attach (its `ack`) before it goes uncounted.
INSTR_ATTACH_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_INSTR_ATTACH_S", "5"))  # env: tests
# Failed attaches in a row after which a server's calls go uncounted.
INSTR_GIVE_UP = 3
HYBRID_EVENTS = ("cpu_core/instructions/u", "cpu_atom/instructions/u")
# CDO_SPEED_PERF: a test hook, the perf command to run (a stub in the tests).
PERF = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_PERF", "perf")


def perf_event_args(events=None):
    """`-e` arguments for `events`: at most ONE event per PMU (`cpu_core/...`,
    `cpu_atom/...`, or the plain `instructions:u`). Instructions use the fixed
    counter, so one event a PMU leaves nothing in this perf run to multiplex: a
    count never comes out short because of our own events (review r1, sol). A
    second event on a PMU is refused here, not added."""
    events = events or INSTR_EVENTS
    pmus = [e.split("/", 1)[0] if "/" in e else "" for e in events]
    if len(pmus) != len(set(pmus)):
        raise ValueError(f"cdo-speed: one perf event per PMU only (no multiplexing): {events}")
    return [a for e in events for a in ("-e", e)]


def instr_total(text, events=None):
    """The summed count of `perf stat -x,` output for `events`, or None. An event
    that never ran (a call that stayed on one core type) reads `<not counted>`:
    0. Any other non-number (`<not supported>`), or an event missing, is None.
    One event per PMU (`perf_event_args`) leaves nothing in this run to
    multiplex. perf's time-share column is no self-check here: on a hybrid CPU a
    core type's event is enabled all along but runs only while the task is on
    that type, so enabled and running differ with no multiplexing at all (58 %
    and 42 % for 4 threads, measured)."""
    events = events or INSTR_EVENTS
    seen, total = set(), 0
    for line in text.splitlines():
        cols = line.strip().split(",")
        if len(cols) > 2 and cols[2] in events:
            if cols[0].isdigit():
                total += int(cols[0])
            elif cols[0] != "<not counted>":
                return None
            seen.add(cols[2])
    return total if seen == set(events) else None


def instr_probe():
    """(events, why): the events that count user-mode instructions here, or ((),
    the reason none can). CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0 turns counting off."""
    if os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_INSTR") == "0":
        return (), "off (CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0)"
    why = "perf gave no count"
    for events in (HYBRID_EVENTS, ("instructions:u",)):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([PERF, "stat", "--no-scale", "-x,", *perf_event_args(events),
                                "--", "true"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            return (), f"perf did not run ({e})"
        if r.returncode == 0 and instr_total(r.stderr, events):
            return events, ""
        why = (r.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {r.returncode}"])[-1]
    return (), f"perf cannot count user-mode instructions here ({why}); scripts/perf-probe"


class Server:
    """One `alsem mcp` server over stdio (newline-delimited JSON-RPC)."""

    def __init__(self, exe, ws, outdir, name):
        self.name = name
        self.root = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=f"cdo-speed-{name}-", dir=outdir)
        self.err = open(os.path.join(outdir, f"{name}.server.err"), "wb")
        self.p = subprocess.Popen(
            [exe, "mcp", "--workspace", ws, "--output-root", self.root],
            stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=self.err, env=child_env())
        self.buf = b""
        self.next_id = 0
        # pid -> the highest VmHWM read for it (kept after the process exits).
        self.hwm_kib = {}
        r = self.request("initialize", {"protocolVersion": "2025-06-18", "capabilities": {},
                                        "clientInfo": {"name": "cdo-speed", "version": "1"}})
        if "result" not in r:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {name}: initialize failed: {r}")
        self.send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": "notifications/initialized", "params": {}})

    def send(self, msg):
        self.p.stdin.write((json.dumps(msg) + "\n").encode())
        self.p.stdin.flush()

    def readline(self, deadline):
        while b"\n" not in self.buf:
            left = deadline - time.monotonic()
            if left <= 0:
                raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: an MCP call timed out")
            ready, _, _ = select.select([self.p.stdout], [], [], left)
            if ready:
                chunk = os.read(self.p.stdout.fileno(), 1 << 20)
                if not chunk:
                    raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: the MCP server closed its stdout")
                self.buf += chunk
        line, self.buf = self.buf.split(b"\n", 1)
        return json.loads(line)

    def request(self, method, params):
        self.next_id += 1
        rid = self.next_id
        self.send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": rid, "method": method, "params": params})
        deadline = time.monotonic() + CALL_TIMEOUT
        while True:
            msg = self.readline(deadline)
            if msg.get("id") == rid:
                return msg

    def cpu(self):
        """CPU seconds of the server and every live engine child (with their
        own waited-for children)."""
        return sum(proc_cpu_seconds(p) for p in [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)])

    def call(self, tool, args):
        """(wall s, CPU s, stdout sha256, exit code, first stderr lines) of one
        tool call; exit code -1 when the engine failed (no CLI exit).
        `self.last_instr` then holds the call's user-mode instructions, or None
        when they are not counted (TLS-83)."""
        perf = self.instr_start()
        # instr_stop runs even when the call times out (SystemExit): perf and
        # its FIFOs never outlive the call (review r1, sol).
        try:
            c0 = self.cpu()
            t0 = time.monotonic()
            r = self.request("tools/call", {"name": tool, "arguments": args})
            wall = time.monotonic() - t0
            cpu = self.cpu() - c0
        finally:
            self.last_instr = self.instr_stop(perf)
        self.sample_hwm()
        if "error" in r:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: {tool}: {r['error']}")
        env = r["result"].get("structuredContent", {})
        if "stdoutFile" in env:
            sha = env["stdoutFile"].get("sha256", "")[:16]
        else:
            text = env.get("stdoutText", env.get("output", ""))
            if not isinstance(text, str):
                text = json.dumps(text, sort_keys=True)
            sha = hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
        err = env.get("stderr") or [env.get("message", "")]
        if isinstance(err, str):
            err = err.splitlines()
        return wall, cpu, sha, env.get("exitCode", -1), [str(x) for x in err if x][:5]

    # ⟨TLS-83⟩ User-mode instructions over a call (INSTR_EVENTS, chosen at the
    # start by instr_probe), from `perf stat` attached to the server and its live
    # children; threads and children they start during the call are counted too
    # (inherit). Unscaled (`--no-scale`): an unpinned thread moves between core
    # types, and perf's default scaling would inflate each type's partial count.
    def instr_start(self):
        # After INSTR_GIVE_UP attaches in a row fail, this server counts no more:
        # each failure costs up to INSTR_ATTACH_S inside the CDO slot, 66 calls a
        # run (review r1, sol).
        if not INSTR_EVENTS or getattr(self, "instr_failures", 0) >= INSTR_GIVE_UP:
            return None
        started = self._instr_start()
        self.instr_failures = 0 if started else getattr(self, "instr_failures", 0) + 1
        if self.instr_failures == INSTR_GIVE_UP:
            print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf failed to attach {INSTR_GIVE_UP} times in a row; "
                  "this server's calls are not counted from here on", file=sys.stderr)
        return started

    def _instr_start(self):
        # Counting must never fail a run (review r1, Fable: a fork that fails
        # while the gate holds 16-28 GB, or a perf gone since the probe): the
        # call is then just not counted.
        # The call starts only once perf says its counters run: perf starts them
        # disabled (`-D -1`), we write `enable` to its control FIFO and wait for
        # its `ack` (at most INSTR_ATTACH_S). A fixed sleep raced a slow perf start
        # under load and counted a call from part-way through (TLS-92's gate: a
        # 0-count call). No ack in time: the call is not counted.
        perf = ctl_fd = ack_fd = None
        try:
            attached = [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)]
            pids = ",".join(str(p) for p in attached)
            work = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="perf-", dir=self.root)
            out, ctl, ack = (os.path.join(work, n) for n in ("out.csv", "ctl", "ack"))
            os.mkfifo(ctl)
            os.mkfifo(ack)
            ack_fd = os.open(ack, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
            perf = subprocess.Popen([PERF, "stat", "--no-scale", "-x,", *perf_event_args(),
                                     "-D", "-1", "--control", f"fifo:{ctl},{ack}",
                                     "-o", out, "-p", pids],
                                    stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            deadline = time.monotonic() + INSTR_ATTACH_S
            # perf opens its end while parsing its options, BEFORE its counters
            # exist: this wait does not mean they run. Only the `ack` below does
            # (review r1, Fable; TLS-83's proof: without the ack wait, red).
            while ctl_fd is None:
                try:
                    ctl_fd = os.open(ctl, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
                except OSError:  # ENXIO: no reader yet
                    if time.monotonic() > deadline or perf.poll() is not None:
                        raise
                    time.sleep(0.005)
            os.write(ctl_fd, b"enable\n")
            got = b""
            while b"ack" not in got:
                left = deadline - time.monotonic()
                if left <= 0 or not select.select([ack_fd], [], [], left)[0]:
                    raise OSError("perf gave no ack")
                chunk = os.read(ack_fd, 64)
                if not chunk and perf.poll() is not None:
                    raise OSError("perf ended before its ack")
                got += chunk
            # A process that appeared between the pid scan and the attach (an
            # engine child restarted) is not counted, inherit or not: such a
            # call's count would be partial (review r1, sol).
            if set(descendants(self.p.pid)) - set(attached):
                raise OSError("a server process started before perf attached")
        except OSError:
            for fd in (ctl_fd, ack_fd):
                if fd is not None:
                    os.close(fd)
            if perf is not None:
                perf.kill()
                try:
                    perf.wait(timeout=10)
                except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                    pass
            return None
        return perf, out, (ctl_fd, ack_fd)

    def instr_stop(self, started):
        """The call's instruction count, or None when perf could not give it (the
        call then has no instruction ratio; CPU and wall still judge it)."""
        if started is None:
            return None
        perf, out, fds = started
        perf.send_signal(signal.SIGINT)
        try:
            perf.wait(timeout=30)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            perf.kill()
            try:
                perf.wait(timeout=10)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                pass
            total = None
        else:
            try:
                with open(out) as f:
                    total = instr_total(f.read())
            except OSError:
                total = None
        for fd in fds:
            os.close(fd)
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.dirname(out), ignore_errors=True)
        # No instruction at all (every event `<not counted>`) is no count: as a 0
        # it would pull the round total down (review r1, Fable).
        return total or None

    def sample_hwm(self):
        """Read the VmHWM of the server and every live child. Called after each
        call, so a child that exits later in the run still counts."""
        for p in [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)]:
            kib = proc_status_kib(p, "VmHWM")
            if kib > self.hwm_kib.get(p, 0):
                self.hwm_kib[p] = kib

    def peak_mib(self):
        """Every server process's peak RSS (VmHWM), summed, including the
        processes that exited during the run."""
        self.sample_hwm()
        return sum(self.hwm_kib.values()) / 1024

    def close(self):
        # ⟨TLS-79⟩ The whole tree must be gone: an engine child left behind keeps
        # its model resident, which is what made a build beside it slower.
        kids = descendants(self.p.pid)
        try:
            self.p.stdin.close()
            self.p.wait(timeout=60)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            self.p.kill()
        deadline = time.monotonic() + 30
        left = [k for k in kids if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        while left and time.monotonic() < deadline:
            time.sleep(0.2)
            left = [k for k in left if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        for k in left:
            log(f"server {self.name}: child {k} still alive 30 s after the close: killed")
            try:
                os.kill(k, 9)
            except OSError:
                pass
        deadline = time.monotonic() + 5
        while left and time.monotonic() < deadline:
            time.sleep(0.1)
            left = [k for k in left if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        for k in left:
            log(f"server {self.name}: child {k} survived SIGKILL: its memory may still be "
                f"resident (the next build's timing may be skewed)")
        self.err.close()
        shutil.rmtree(self.root, ignore_errors=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class CallFailed(Exception):
    """A timed call that did not exit 0: (what, its first stderr lines)."""


def timed_call(server, side, cid, tool, args, rnd, warm_pass, tag, records):
    """One timed call on `side`'s server, logged and recorded."""
    load = load_1min()
    pressure, _ = cpu_pressure()
    wall, cpu, sha, code, err = server.call(tool, args)
    exit_note = f"  exit {code}" if code != 0 else ""
    shown = "?" if pressure is None else f"{pressure:.1f}"
    instr = getattr(server, "last_instr", None)
    instr_note = "" if instr is None else f"  instr {instr / 1e9:8.3f}e9"
    log(f"  {tag} {cid:<12} {side:<5} wall {wall:7.2f} s  cpu {cpu:7.2f} s"
        f"  load {load:5.1f}  pressure {shown:>5}{instr_note}{exit_note}")
    if code != 0:
        raise CallFailed(f"{tag} {cid} ({tool}) on {side} exited {code}", err)
    records.append(dict(call=cid, side=side, round=rnd, warm_pass=warm_pass,
                        wall=wall, cpu=cpu, sha=sha, load=load, pressure=pressure,
                        instr=instr))


def run_round(rnd, servers, records, warm_pass=0, skip=0):
    """One round's calls (`skip`: leave out the first calls of the warm-up, done
    already)."""
    order = ["base", "head"] if rnd % 2 == 0 else ["head", "base"]
    if rnd == 0:
        calls = WARM_UP
    else:
        calls = calls_for(ROUND_ROUTINES[rnd - 1])
        log(f"round {rnd}: {ROUND_ROUTINES[rnd - 1]}")
    for i, (cid, tool, args) in enumerate(calls):
        if i < skip:
            continue
        # The warm-up flips the order per call, and its second pass (fresh
        # servers) flips every call again: each warm-up call is timed once with
        # each side second (measured: going second costs up to ~15 % under load).
        side_order = order if rnd != 0 or i % 2 == 0 else order[::-1]
        if rnd == 0 and warm_pass % 2 == 1:
            side_order = side_order[::-1]
        tag = f"r0.{warm_pass + 1}" if rnd == 0 else f"r{rnd}"
        for side in side_order:
            timed_call(servers[side], side, cid, tool, args, rnd, warm_pass, tag, records)


def judge(records):
    """Per call: (rows, the calls too noisy to judge)."""
    rows, noisy = [], []
    calls = list(dict.fromkeys(r["call"] for r in records))
    rounds = sorted({r["round"] for r in records if r["round"] > 0})
    # The round total: every counted call's time summed per round and side.
    for side in ("base", "head"):
        for rnd in rounds:
            rs = [r for r in records if r["round"] == rnd and r["side"] == side]
            instr = [r.get("instr") for r in rs]
            records.append(dict(call="round-total", side=side, round=rnd, total=True,
                                wall=sum(r["wall"] for r in rs),
                                cpu=sum(r["cpu"] for r in rs), sha="",
                                instr=sum(instr) if instr and None not in instr else None))
    calls.append("round-total")
    for cid in calls:
        rs = [r for r in records if r["call"] == cid]
        a = {r["round"]: r for r in rs if r["side"] == "base"}
        b = {r["round"]: r for r in rs if r["side"] == "head"}
        paired = sorted(set(a) & set(b))
        row = dict(call=cid, rounds=paired, same_output=(
            [a[k]["sha"] for k in paired] == [b[k]["sha"] for k in paired]))
        # ⟨TLS-83⟩ Instructions only where every paired call has a count.
        counted = paired and all((a[k].get("instr") or 0) > 0 and (b[k].get("instr") or 0) > 0
                                 for k in paired)
        for m in ("cpu", "wall", "instr") if counted else ("cpu", "wall"):
            ratios = [b[k][m] / a[k][m] if a[k][m] > 0 else 1.0 for k in paired]
            row[m] = dict(base=statistics.median(a[k][m] for k in paired),
                          head=statistics.median(b[k][m] for k in paired),
                          ratio=statistics.median(ratios), ratios=ratios,
                          spread=(max(ratios) / min(ratios)) if min(ratios) > 0 else 9.9)
        row["judged"] = row["cpu"]["base"] >= MIN_SECONDS
        rows.append(row)
        # Undecided: the rounds disagree about the verdict (some ratios over
        # the limit, some not). The ratios differ by routine on a real change
        # too (round 4's slowdown cost each routine its own number of
        # searches), so a wide spread alone is not noise.
        if row["judged"] and any(
                min(row[m]["ratios"]) <= lim < max(row[m]["ratios"])
                for m, lim in (("cpu", CPU_LIMIT), ("wall", WALL_LIMIT))):
            noisy.append(cid)
    return rows, noisy


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("workspace", nargs="?")
    ap.add_argument("--warm", metavar="REV",
                    help="only build REV's binary into the cache (as a gate would build its base) "
                         "and exit; no workspace, no timing (TLS-18: run after a landing)")
    ap.add_argument("--base", help="the base commit (default: merge-base HEAD master)")
    ap.add_argument("--head", help="a commit to time instead of the working tree")
    ap.add_argument("--base-exe", help="a built base binary (noise measurements; no build)")
    ap.add_argument("--head-exe", help="a built head binary (noise measurements; no build)")
    ap.add_argument("--rounds", type=int, default=3,
                    help=f"timed rounds, 1 to {len(ROUND_ROUTINES)} (one new routine per round; "
                         "default 3)")
    ap.add_argument("--max-rounds", type=int, default=len(ROUND_ROUTINES))
    ap.add_argument("--out", help="the folder for outputs and speed.json")
    ap.add_argument("--build-only", action="store_true", help="build both binaries, time nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--no-build", action="store_true",
                    help="the working tree's release-fast alsem is already built "
                         "(in <target>/gate-release, where --build-only builds it)")
    ap.add_argument("--warm-samples", type=int, default=WARM_SAMPLES, choices=[1, 2],
                    help="warm-up passes on fresh servers, the second flipped (default 2; "
                         "1 = one pass, base built first: a quick look, biased under load)")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    # TLS-25: each round times a routine no round timed before, so there are at most as many
    # rounds as routines. Refused here, before anything is built or started: a run asked for
    # more once timed the warm-up and every round (minutes on CDO), then stopped with an
    # IndexError and wrote no speed.json.
    if not 1 <= args.rounds <= len(ROUND_ROUTINES):
        ap.error(f"--rounds {args.rounds}: give 1 to {len(ROUND_ROUTINES)}, one round per "
                 f"routine in ROUND_ROUTINES (a routine timed again measures nothing new)")
    args.max_rounds = min(max(args.max_rounds, args.rounds), len(ROUND_ROUTINES))

    if not sys.platform.startswith("linux"):
        log("this gate reads /proc: Linux only")
        return 2
    repo = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
    if args.warm:
        # The gate's own builder and cache key, so the next gate finds exactly the binary it
        # would have built (and waits on the cache lock if this build is still running).
        os.makedirs(cache_root(), exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(cache_root(), "warm.log"), "a") as logf:
            exe, sha = cached_binary(repo, args.warm, logf)
        log(f"warm: {args.warm} = {sha[:10]} cached at {exe}")
        return 0
    if not args.workspace:
        log("a workspace is required (or --warm REV)")
        return 2
    ws = os.path.abspath(args.workspace)
    if not os.path.isdir(ws):
        log(f"no workspace at {ws}")
        return 2
    outdir = os.path.abspath(args.out or os.path.join(
        target_dir(repo), "cdo-speed-runs", time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S") + f"-{os.getpid()}"))
    os.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)
    log(f"outputs in {outdir}")

    t_start = time.monotonic()
    with open(os.path.join(outdir, "build.log"), "a") as logf:
        if args.base_exe:
            base_rev, base_exe, base_sha = args.base_exe, args.base_exe, "given-binary"
        else:
            base_rev = args.base or default_base(repo, args.head)
            base_exe, base_sha = cached_binary(repo, base_rev, logf)
        if args.head_exe:
            head_exe, head_label = args.head_exe, args.head_exe
        elif args.head:
            head_exe, head_sha = cached_binary(repo, args.head, logf)
            head_label = head_sha[:10]
        else:
            # TLS-34: the sources' key before the build, checked again after it.
            # One cdo-speed at a time builds into this folder, snapshots what it
            # built and copies its timed head (review r3-r4, sol: two concurrent
            # runs could swap the binary between one's build and its copies).
            os.makedirs(gate_release_dir(repo), exist_ok=True)
            with open(os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), ".cdo-speed-build.lock"),
                      "w") as build_lock:
                fcntl.flock(build_lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
                key_before = None if args.no_build else working_tree_key(repo)
                built = (os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), "release-fast", "alsem")
                         if args.no_build else build_working_tree(repo, logf))
                if not os.path.exists(built):
                    log(f"no binary at {built}")
                    return 2
                if not args.no_build:
                    record_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before)
                # A copy: a later build into the target folder cannot swap it mid-run.
                head_exe = os.path.join(outdir, "head-alsem")
                if not args.build_only:
                    shutil.copy2(built, head_exe)
            dirty = git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no")
            head_label = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--short=10", "HEAD") + (" + edits" if dirty else "")
    t_built = time.monotonic()
    if args.build_only:
        log(f"built (no timing asked) in {t_built - t_start:.0f} s")
        return 0
    timed_hold(repo)
    global INSTR_EVENTS, INSTR_WHY
    INSTR_EVENTS, INSTR_WHY = instr_probe()
    if INSTR_EVENTS:
        log(f"instructions: counted per call ({', '.join(INSTR_EVENTS)}); advisory limit "
            f"x{INSTR_LIMIT}")
    else:
        log(f"instructions: NOT counted, CPU and wall only: {INSTR_WHY}")

    log(f"base {base_sha[:10]} ({base_rev}) against head {head_label}; workspace {ws}")
    hooks = [f"{k}={os.environ[k]}" for k in TEST_HOOKS if k in os.environ]
    if hooks:
        log(f"test hooks set (a real gate sets none): {' '.join(hooks)}")
    load0 = load_1min()
    pressure0, _ = cpu_pressure()
    records = []
    servers = {}
    # Per warm-up pass: (the host's pressure then, admitted only by the timeout).
    calm = []
    # Per timed block (each warm-up pass, the rounds): (name, its stall share).
    blocks = []
    cur = None
    # The peak RSS over every server pair (the warm-up passes start fresh ones).
    peaks = {"base": 0.0, "head": 0.0}
    try:
        for p in range(args.warm_samples):
            calm.append(wait_calm(f"warm-up {p + 1} of {args.warm_samples}"))
            # The pass's stall share also covers its untimed rebuild (TLS-79).
            cur = (f"warm-up {p + 1}", Stalls())
            exes = {"base": base_exe, "head": head_exe}
            first = "base" if p % 2 == 0 else "head"
            second = "head" if first == "base" else "base"
            log(f"round 0, warm-up {p + 1} of {args.warm_samples} (fresh servers; "
                f"{first} builds first)")
            # ⟨TLS-79⟩ Each side's model build is timed with the other side's
            # server closed: with the first side's model resident (its server
            # idle, 0.00-0.01 s CPU), the second build cost x1.06-x1.29 CPU in
            # six pairs of one binary against itself, and x0.85-x0.99 with the
            # first server closed (2026-11-15). Then the first side is started
            # again and builds untimed, so the remaining warm-up calls and the
            # rounds have both servers.
            _, b_tool, b_args = WARM_UP[0]
            tag = f"r0.{p + 1}"
            servers[first] = Server(exes[first], ws, outdir, f"{first}{p + 1}")
            timed_call(servers[first], first, "build", b_tool, b_args, 0, p, tag, records)
            peaks[first] = max(peaks[first], servers[first].peak_mib())
            servers.pop(first).close()
            servers[second] = Server(exes[second], ws, outdir, f"{second}{p + 1}")
            timed_call(servers[second], second, "build", b_tool, b_args, 0, p, tag, records)
            servers[first] = Server(exes[first], ws, outdir, f"{first}{p + 1}-again")
            wall, cpu, _, code, err = servers[first].call(b_tool, b_args)
            log(f"  {tag} {'build':<12} {first:<5} wall {wall:7.2f} s  cpu {cpu:7.2f} s"
                f"  (again, untimed: the server the remaining calls use)")
            if code != 0:
                raise CallFailed(f"{tag} build (again) on {first} exited {code}", err)
            run_round(0, servers, records, warm_pass=p, skip=1)
            blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
            cur = None
            if p + 1 < args.warm_samples:
                for side, s in servers.items():
                    peaks[side] = max(peaks[side], s.peak_mib())
                    s.close()
                servers = {}
        cur = ("rounds", Stalls())
        rnd = 0
        while True:
            rnd += 1
            run_round(rnd, servers, records)
            if rnd < args.rounds:
                continue
            _, noisy = judge([dict(r) for r in records if r["round"] > 0])
            if noisy and rnd < args.max_rounds:
                log(f"the rounds disagree about {', '.join(noisy)}: one more round")
                continue
            break
        blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
        cur = None
        for side in servers:
            peaks[side] = max(peaks[side], servers[side].peak_mib())
    except CallFailed as e:
        what, err = e.args
        if cur is not None:
            blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
        log(f"FAIL: {what}; nothing is judged on a failed call")
        for line in err or ["(no stderr)"]:
            log(f"  | {line}")
        label = under_load_label(calm, blocks)
        if label:
            log(f"FAIL{label}")
        return 1
    finally:
        for s in servers.values():
            s.close()

    warm_records = [r for r in records if r["round"] == 0]
    rows, noisy = judge([r for r in records if r["round"] > 0])
    # The warm-up's calls: one sample per pass, paired by pass; the ratio is
    # the geometric mean over the passes (with the two flipped ones, a factor k
    # on one and 1/k on the other cancels exactly; an arithmetic mean would
    # lean against head).
    warm_rows, _ = judge([dict(r, round=r["warm_pass"] + 1) for r in warm_records])
    warm_rows = [r for r in warm_rows if r["call"] != "round-total"]
    for row in warm_rows:
        for m in ("cpu", "wall", "instr"):
            if m not in row:
                continue
            ratios = row[m]["ratios"]
            if ratios and min(ratios) > 0:
                row[m]["ratio"] = statistics.geometric_mean(ratios)
        loads = [r["load"] for r in warm_records if r["call"] == row["call"]]
        row["load"] = dict(min=min(loads), max=max(loads))
    failed, advisories = [], []
    log(f"verdict, head/base (limits: rounds cpu {CPU_LIMIT} and wall {WALL_LIMIT} on the "
        f"median ratio, warm-up cpu {WARM_CPU_LIMIT} and wall {WARM_WALL_LIMIT}, memory "
        f"{MEM_LIMIT}; a call is judged "
        f"alone when its base CPU is at least {MIN_SECONDS} s):")
    for row in warm_rows:
        row["warm"] = True
        row["judged"] = row["cpu"]["base"] >= WARM_MIN_SECONDS
    for row in warm_rows + rows:
        c, w = row["cpu"], row["wall"]
        warm = row.get("warm", False)
        note = "" if row["judged"] else (
            "  (reported only)" if warm else "  (in the round total only)")
        if row["call"] in noisy:
            note += "  (rounds disagree: the median decides)"
        if not row["same_output"] and row["call"] != "round-total":
            note += "  [outputs or exit codes differ]"
        if warm:
            note += (f"  (x{'/x'.join(f'{x:.3f}' for x in c['ratios'])} by pass; load "
                     f"{row['load']['min']:.1f}-{row['load']['max']:.1f})")
        i = row.get("instr")
        instr_col = f"   instr x{i['ratio']:.4f}" if i else ""
        log(f"  {row['call']:<14} cpu {c['base']:7.2f} -> {c['head']:7.2f} s x{c['ratio']:.3f}"
            f"   wall {w['base']:7.2f} -> {w['head']:7.2f} s x{w['ratio']:.3f}"
            f"{instr_col}   spread {c['spread']:.2f}/{w['spread']:.2f}{note}")
        if not row["judged"]:
            continue
        # ⟨TLS-83⟩ Advisory: reported, never failing (see INSTR_LIMIT), on the
        # judged rows only: below the CPU-time cutoff a call is a few thousand
        # instructions whose ratio swings (8 false advisories in a run of one
        # binary against itself when tried on every row; review r1, sol).
        if i and i["ratio"] > INSTR_LIMIT:
            advisories.append(f"{row['call']}: instructions x{i['ratio']:.4f} "
                              f"(advisory limit {INSTR_LIMIT}{', warm-up' if warm else ''})")
        if warm:
            if c["ratio"] > WARM_CPU_LIMIT:
                failed.append(f"{row['call']}: CPU time x{c['ratio']:.2f} "
                              f"(limit {WARM_CPU_LIMIT}, warm-up, {len(c['ratios'])} pass(es))")
            if w["ratio"] > WARM_WALL_LIMIT:
                failed.append(f"{row['call']}: wall time x{w['ratio']:.2f} "
                              f"(limit {WARM_WALL_LIMIT}, warm-up, {len(c['ratios'])} pass(es); "
                              f"lost parallelism?)")
            continue
        if c["ratio"] > CPU_LIMIT:
            failed.append(f"{row['call']}: CPU time x{c['ratio']:.2f} (limit {CPU_LIMIT})")
        if w["ratio"] > WALL_LIMIT:
            failed.append(f"{row['call']}: wall time x{w['ratio']:.2f} (limit {WALL_LIMIT})")
    mem_ratio = peaks["head"] / peaks["base"] if peaks["base"] else 9.9
    log(f"  {'peak memory':<14} {peaks['base']:.0f} -> {peaks['head']:.0f} MiB x{mem_ratio:.3f}"
        " (every server process's VmHWM, summed)")
    if mem_ratio > MEM_LIMIT:
        failed.append(f"peak memory x{mem_ratio:.2f} (limit {MEM_LIMIT})")
    # ⟨TLS-83⟩ The instruction ratios on the verdict line (cdo-gate's summary
    # quotes it): the model build (warm-up) and the round total.
    if INSTR_EVENTS:
        shown = [f"{r['call']} x{r['instr']['ratio']:.4f}"
                 for r in warm_rows + rows if r["call"] in ("build", "round-total") and "instr" in r]
        instr_text = (f"instructions: {', '.join(shown) or 'no call fully counted'}; "
                      f"advisory limit x{INSTR_LIMIT}"
                      f"{', ADVISORY: ' + str(len(advisories)) + ' over' if advisories else ''}")
    else:
        instr_text = "instructions: not counted, CPU and wall only"
    for line in advisories:
        log(f"ADVISORY (not failing): {line}")
    elapsed = time.monotonic() - t_start
    label = under_load_label(calm, blocks)
    under = [i + 1 for i, (_, loaded) in enumerate(calm) if loaded]
    unknown = any(loaded is None for _, loaded in calm) or any(s is None for _, s in blocks)
    with open(os.path.join(outdir, "speed.json"), "w") as f:
        json.dump(dict(base=base_sha, head=head_label, workspace=ws, rounds=rnd,
                       limits=dict(cpu=CPU_LIMIT, wall=WALL_LIMIT, mem=MEM_LIMIT,
                                   warm_cpu=WARM_CPU_LIMIT, warm_wall=WARM_WALL_LIMIT,
                                   min_seconds=MIN_SECONDS, warm_min_seconds=WARM_MIN_SECONDS),
                       build_s=t_built - t_start, timing_s=elapsed - (t_built - t_start),
                       peaks_mib=peaks, warm_rows=warm_rows, rows=rows, records=records,
                       failed=failed, loadavg_start=load0, loadavg_end=load_1min(),
                       pressure_start=pressure0, pressure_end=cpu_pressure()[0],
                       calm_pressure=CALM_PRESSURE, admitted_by_timeout=under,
                       stall_shares=dict(blocks), under_load=bool(label.startswith(" UNDER")),
                       pressure_unknown=unknown, warm_samples=args.warm_samples,
                       instructions=dict(events=list(INSTR_EVENTS), why_not=INSTR_WHY,
                                         advisory_limit=INSTR_LIMIT, advisories=advisories)),
                  f, indent=1)
    log(f"build {t_built - t_start:.0f} s, timing {elapsed - (t_built - t_start):.0f} s, "
        f"load average {load0:.1f} at the start, {load_1min():.1f} at the end; "
        f"CPU stall share of this container: {shares_text(blocks)}")
    if failed:
        for line in failed:
            log(f"FAIL: {line}")
        # After the failures, not in parentheses: it must not read as the reason.
        log(f"FAIL{label}; {instr_text}")
        return 1
    log(f"PASS{label} ({instr_text})")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except SystemExit as e:
        if isinstance(e.code, str):  # a "cannot run" message
            print(e.code, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            sys.exit(2)
        raise
